<section id="sec-mistakes" class="col">
  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul class="tight">
    <li><strong>Hz used as rad/s.</strong> \(k = m\omega_n^2\) needs \(\omega_n\) in rad/s. With \(f_n\) in Hz the stiffness comes out \(4\pi^2\), about 39 times, too small. You catch it by asking what the number counts. A frequency read off a record counts cycles, and it is multiplied by \(2\pi\) before it meets \(k\) or \(m\).</li>
    <li><strong>Tonnes used as kilograms.</strong> A factor 1000 in mass is a factor of about 32 in frequency. You catch it by the size of the result. A tower top of hundreds of tonnes swings once every few seconds, not several times a second.</li>
    <li><strong>The decay over many cycles taken as \(\delta\).</strong> The logarithm of the peak ratio over ten cycles is ten times \(\delta\), and the damping ratio comes out ten times too large. You catch it by that damping ratio. A parked steel tower has a few percent at most, and a value above ten percent is a sign the cycle count was forgotten.</li>
    <li><strong>Damping ratio entered in percent.</strong> \(\zeta\) = 1 % is 0.01 in every formula. Entered as 1, the formulas give critical damping, a tower that creeps back without a single swing. You catch it by the kind of motion. A structure that rings has \(\zeta\) far below 1.</li>
    <li><strong>Only the nacelle and rotor counted as the mass.</strong> The tower moves too, most of all near the top, and part of its mass swings with the top. Leave it out and the frequency comes out too high. You catch it by asking what moves. For a uniform tower about a quarter of its own mass belongs in \(m\), see Limits.</li>
    <li><strong>Zero crossings counted as cycles.</strong> The record crosses zero twice per cycle. Counting each crossing as a cycle halves the period and doubles the frequency. You catch it by counting peaks on one side of zero, one per cycle.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

